ch listening on port 8080\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"systemd unit inventory-app was created, enabled, and started on both node2 and node3 (cmd-a1581f74bd3d4ac3, cmd-5f6fac5d836542c8). Node1 confirmed reachability to both backends' /healthz endpoints returning correct per-node identity (cmd-d32fd8d3f717438b). Post-executor lifecycle snapshot independently confirms python3 listening on 0.0.0.0:8080 on both node2 and node3 (global:after_executor:node2:listening-sockets, global:after_executor:node3:listening-sockets). Data served via /api/inventory verified with correct content on both nodes (cmd-316abd0c22b74bd2, cmd-ba497c27a8c54d02).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-a1581f74bd3d4ac3\",\"cmd-5f6fac5d836542c8\",\"cmd-d32fd8d3f717438b\",\"global:after_executor:node2:listening-sockets\",\"global:after_executor:node3:listening-sockets\",\"cmd-316abd0c22b74bd2\",\"cmd-ba497c27a8c54d02\"]},{\"id\":\"req-2\",\"requirement\":\"node1 configured to load-balance incoming HTTP requests across both backends on port 80\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"nginx installed and configured with an upstream block round-robining across 10.100.47.11:8080 and 10.100.47.12:8080, listening on port 80 (cmd-735b9ad4ac0e4aeb, cmd-82789aec952e4497). A conflicting default server block on port 80 was removed to avoid ambiguity (cmd-d903504f5c1b4af1). After diagnosing and fixing an SELinux boolean (httpd_can_network_connect) that was blocking proxying, repeated curl requests to node1:80/api/inventory alternated correctly between node2 and node3 responses with 200 status (cmd-5c9a826064cb4a1c, cmd-19b752536ccc46a8). Post-executor snapshot independently confirms nginx listening on 0.0.0.0:80 on node1 (global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-735b9ad4ac0e4aeb\",\"cmd-82789aec952e4497\",\"cmd-d903504f5c1b4af1\",\"cmd-5c9a826064cb4a1c\",\"cmd-19b752536ccc46a8\",\"global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets\"]},{\"id\":\"req-3\",\"requirement\":\"Provider-wide node-restart evidence protocol executed on every managed node (node1, node2, node3) with pre-restart baseline, restart, changed boot identity, and recovered service/data/cluster state\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"For all three nodes the trace shows a healthy pre-restart baseline (cmd-2d2e1983acd945db for node2, cmd-95fa289c782c4e95 for node3, cmd-03abd16d43b4470f for node1), a restart acknowledgement via systemctl reboot (cmd-7e00016f596b46f0, cmd-7f5a7dfcaf1c4e61, cmd-fc73f896b67d44d3), a directly observed change in boot_id (cmd-bb13adf05b7d4250, cmd-ff9a11305d0641ae, cmd-bcc61a6bf9394352, the latter following observed connection failures during node1's actual downtime), and post-restart recovery of service, data, and cluster-wide load-balancing behavior (cmd-316abd0c22b74bd2/cmd-b2c8a34ad39148f3 for node2, cmd-ba497c27a8c54d02/cmd-45ce457c032a477b for node3, cmd-34b84a56851448e6/cmd-df94acbe6172445f for node1). This is independently corroborated by the fixed after-prepare vs after-executor boot-identity cross-check, which shows differing boot IDs for all three nodes.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-2d2e1983acd945db\",\"cmd-95fa289c782c4e95\",\"cmd-03abd16d43b4470f\",\"cmd-7e00016f596b46f0\",\"cmd-7f5a7dfcaf1c4e61\",\"cmd-fc73f896b67d44d3\",\"cmd-bb13adf05b7d4250\",\"cmd-ff9a11305d0641ae\",\"cmd-bcc61a6bf9394352\",\"cmd-316abd0c22b74bd2\",\"cmd-b2c8a34ad39148f3\",\"cmd-ba497c27a8c54d02\",\"cmd-45ce457c032a477b\",\"cmd-34b84a56851448e6\",\"cmd-df94acbe6172445f\"]}],\"confidence\":0.9,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.85,\"summary\":\"Reviewing the executor's chronological command trace, all state-changing actions map clearly to task requirements or restoration: package installs (nginx, cmd-a440874c4e624a03), app deployment and systemd unit creation on node2/node3, firewalld port openings, the nginx.conf edit to remove a conflicting default server block (with a backup taken first via 'cp /etc/nginx/nginx.conf /etc/nginx/nginx.conf.orig' in cmd-d903504f5c1b4af1, a bounded, reversible action), and the SELinux boolean fix (cmd-5c9a826064cb4a1c) needed to make the load balancer actually functional after a genuine 502 failure was diagnosed. No experimental or abandoned mutations were found; the two 502-Bad-Gateway curl commands (cmd-fc80891b660a40c7, cmd-50135d5a6d5b434c) were read-only diagnostics, not mutations. Minor unaddressed residue: the /etc/nginx/nginx.conf.orig backup file was left on node1 and /tmp/reboot.log files (created by the backgrounded 'nohup sh -c reboot' commands) were left on all three nodes post-restart, visible in the after-executor temporary-files snapshot; these are low-risk, non-conflicting artifacts and do not indicate broken state.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-d903504f5c1b4af1\",\"cmd-5c9a826064cb4a1c\",\"cmd-a440874c4e624a03\",\"global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node3:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node2:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node3:failed-services\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"Both material outcomes of the task, the inventory app running on node2/node3 on port 8080, and nginx on node1 load-balancing round-robin across both backends on port 80, are satisfied with direct, corroborated evidence including independent lifecycle snapshots. The mandatory provider-wide restart protocol was also executed and evidenced completely for all three nodes, with clear before/restart/after boot-identity transitions and confirmed recovery of app, LB, and firewall state. Operational hygiene is good: the one required conflicting-config mutation was backed up before modification, the necessary SELinux fix was applied only after root-causing a genuine failure, and only minor residue (a backup file and reboot logs) was left behind.\",\"limitations\":[\"Executor claims of 'verified' round-robin behavior rely on curl loops issued from node1 itself rather than from 